WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.169 --> 00:00:06.200
Okay, let's start to wrap things up with a few final recommendations.

2
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:09.279
And we'll begin with Project Organization.

3
00:00:09.279 --> 00:00:13.320
Right off the bat, I want to emphasize just how important Project Organization is in

4
00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:15.060
your production.

5
00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:19.519
Without it, things can get really messy very fast, so I highly recommend creating

6
00:00:19.519 --> 00:00:25.160
nested folders and folder structures inside your Content Browser, and inside your Outliner

7
00:00:25.160 --> 00:00:31.449
to maintain Project Organization as you work collaboratively in a production environment.

8
00:00:31.449 --> 00:00:37.109
Typically, the studio you are working for will set this organizational standard.

9
00:00:37.109 --> 00:00:41.909
However, when working on a production, it's recommended that you implement a level and

10
00:00:41.909 --> 00:00:48.460
sub-level hierarchy that will allow for better organization and collaborative compartmentalization.

11
00:00:48.460 --> 00:00:53.020
So your environment, or your persistent level, will contain anything that is universally

12
00:00:53.020 --> 00:00:56.340
shared with the entire project.

13
00:00:56.340 --> 00:01:00.439
Project sets or environments can live on their own sub-levels, and to clarify, when I'm

14
00:01:00.479 --> 00:01:03.759
talking about levels, I'm referring to maps or worlds.

15
00:01:03.759 --> 00:01:06.000
I do not mean level sequences.

16
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:09.060
There is a difference between these two assets.

17
00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:12.859
Levels get organized into sub-levels and live under your persistent level depending

18
00:01:12.859 --> 00:01:15.900
on what is actually needed for your project or departments.

19
00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:20.519
For example, your main lighting setup should live on your persistent level, as it will

20
00:01:20.519 --> 00:01:24.099
be utilized by the entire project or production.

21
00:01:24.099 --> 00:01:30.140
Any level sequence-specific lighting setups should live in a Sequencer Lighting Comp sub-level,

22
00:01:30.140 --> 00:01:34.700
so that anything specific to a level sequence that is spawnable should be connected to that

23
00:01:34.700 --> 00:01:36.890
specific sub-level.

24
00:01:36.890 --> 00:01:40.450
That way, it can get called in and out without affecting the persistent level and

25
00:01:40.450 --> 00:01:45.409
causing problems between users working with that persistent level by having the Sequencer-specific

26
00:01:45.409 --> 00:01:50.980
lighting turn on or off when that sub-level is not active.

27
00:01:50.980 --> 00:01:54.939
Another actor class that has a profound impact on the setting up of action in Unreal

28
00:01:54.939 --> 00:01:57.780
Engine is the use of a Subscene Track.

29
00:01:57.780 --> 00:02:03.060
A Subscene Track essentially references an existing level sequence for the express purpose

30
00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:07.040
of layering multiple actions together.

31
00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:12.099
This type of organization allows you to create departmentalized level sequence organization

32
00:02:12.099 --> 00:02:17.340
so that teams of artists can work together in real-time to construct distinct portions

33
00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:23.000
of the action-based department or discipline and can be utilized in conjunction with

34
00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.210
sub-level organization.

35
00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:31.610
Just as we discussed for the lighting scenarios, cinematography can also do this for cine cameras,

36
00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:35.849
character animation can have its own sub-scene, so all your characters and animations can

37
00:02:35.849 --> 00:02:39.930
live on in an animation sub-level as well.

38
00:02:39.930 --> 00:02:44.770
Level blueprints or level sequence blueprints, level sequence-specific effects, anything that

39
00:02:44.770 --> 00:02:49.409
could be spawnable or triggered can also live on a sub-level under the persistent

40
00:02:49.409 --> 00:02:54.449
level and also be organized in Sequencer through sub-scenes.

41
00:02:54.449 --> 00:02:59.129
All of which can be checked out and shared between the teams and their members in a

42
00:02:59.129 --> 00:03:03.669
production environment through your version control solution.

43
00:03:03.669 --> 00:03:05.069
That's it for this section.

44
00:03:05.069 --> 00:03:09.550
In our next section, we will continue with a few more of our final recommendations.
